#{[{:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120641} :activity/id #uuid "0ef52fb7-e1f4-5106-bb36-6b94715ae636" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048616 :topic/id #uuid "aef82e82-0717-5dbd-b71e-34fa8441f954" :topic/math-academy-id 1795} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120641 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186121148 :tutorial/content "The **principal normal vector** to a curve $C$ at a point $P$ is a unit vector that \n\n- is perpendicular to the unit tangent vector, and\n\n- points in the direction that the curve is bending.\n\nIn general, if the unit tangent vector is given by $\\mathbf T(t),$ then the principal normal vector is given by\n\n$$\n\\mathbf N(t)= \\dfrac{\\mathbf T'(t)}{\\|\\mathbf T'(t)\\|},\n$$\n\nwhere it is assumed that $\\|\\mathbf T'(t)\\|\\neq 0.$\n\nTo demonstrate, suppose that the curve $C$ is parametrized by the vector function \n\n$$\n\\mathbf f(t) = \\left\\langle 2\\cos t, \\: 2\\sin t \\right\\rangle, \\qquad t \\in [0, \\pi].\n$$\n\nWe will compute the principal normal vector at $t=\\dfrac{\\pi}{4}.$\n\nFirst, using the formula for the unit tangent, $\\mathbf T(t) = \\dfrac{\\mathbf f'(t)}{||\\mathbf f'(t)||},$ we find that the general unit tangent vector is\n\n$$\n\\mathbf T(t)= \\left\\langle {-\\sin t}, \\cos t\\right\\rangle.\n$$\n\nNow, when $t=\\dfrac{\\pi}{4},$ we have the point $P(\\sqrt{2},\\sqrt{2}).$ The unit tangent vector $\\mathbf{T}\\left(\\dfrac{\\pi}{4}\\right)$ and the principal normal vector $\\mathbf{N}\\left(\\dfrac{\\pi}{4}\\right)$ at this point are shown below:\n\n![](images/d0/d0a30a44bb825a8a12ca54153b6223b9bf0c2b754245b21650a5664db5e1647e.png)\n\nTo calculate the principal normal vector, we start by calculating the derivative of the unit tangent function. This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T(t) & =⟨-\\operatorname{sin}t,\\operatorname{cos}t⟩ \\\\ {T}^{'}(t) & =⟨-\\operatorname{cos}t,\\,-\\operatorname{sin}t⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general principal normal vector is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}N(t) & =\\frac{{T}^{'}(t)}{||{T}^{'}(t)||} \\\\  & =\\frac{⟨-\\operatorname{cos}t,\\,-\\operatorname{sin}t⟩}{\\sqrt{(-\\operatorname{cos}t)^{2}+(\\operatorname{sin}t)^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{⟨-\\operatorname{cos}t,\\,-\\operatorname{sin}t⟩}{\\sqrt{{cos}^{2}t+{sin}^{2}t}} \\\\  & =\\frac{⟨-\\operatorname{cos}t,\\,-\\operatorname{sin}t⟩}{\\sqrt{1}} \\\\  & =⟨-\\operatorname{cos}t,\\,-\\operatorname{sin}t⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now find $\\mathbf N\\left(\\dfrac\\pi 4\\right),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}N(\\frac{\\pi }{4}) & =⟨-\\operatorname{cos}(\\frac{\\pi }{4}),\\,-\\operatorname{sin}(\\frac{\\pi }{4})⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2},-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, it's easy to check that $\\mathbf T'(t)$ and $\\mathbf N'(t)$ are perpendicular for all $t$ by computing the dot product and showing that we get zero:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T(t)\\cdot N(t) & =⟨-\\operatorname{sin}t,\\operatorname{cos}t⟩\\cdot ⟨-\\operatorname{cos}t,\\,-\\operatorname{sin}t⟩ \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}t\\operatorname{cos}t-\\operatorname{sin}t\\operatorname{cos}t \\\\  & =0\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "03709847-4a63-5975-b20a-1d6693ceadb3" :tutorial/math-academy-id 9376 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "c3443d00-c515-5a63-93a0-747d76d2271c" :step/math-academy-id 164579 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120642}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120642 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119785 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119532 :question/id #uuid "8cb590c5-586d-56a1-af70-d693ea055419" :question/math-academy-id "e-14996"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "930466fc-c7fe-51a5-8634-818abddd0da9" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050685}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120242} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120252} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262761} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186311553} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186344986} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186398305}] :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Principal Normal at a Given Point"} :step/id #uuid "c9c3f801-f35a-5089-8d66-01d0fd9903d2" :step/math-academy-id 164580 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120643}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120643 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119786 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119533 :question/id #uuid "2f92f568-0bf5-58bd-87ce-e6be8d05fe69" :question/math-academy-id "e-14997"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "8d21e1c3-1529-5471-a1e6-76f174da79c3" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120163} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120164} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186290398} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186290399} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186290400} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186290401} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186290402} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186323724} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186323725}] :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Principal Normal at an Arbitrary Point"} :step/id #uuid "812571c6-b194-5ab1-b16a-bcf2ac6ffbe9" :step/math-academy-id 164583 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120644}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120644 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186121149 :tutorial/content "Let $\\mathbf T(t)$ be the unit tangent vector to some differentiable vector-valued function. Since $||\\mathbf T(t)|| = 1,$ and $\\mathbf T(t)$ is obviously parallel to itself, we must have\n\n$$\n\\mathbf T(t) \\cdot \\mathbf T(t) = 1.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating the above relationship with respect to $t,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\mathbf T'(t) \\cdot \\mathbf T(t) + \\mathbf T(t) \\cdot \\mathbf T'(t) = 0,\n$$\n\nwhich can be simplified as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{T}^{'}(t)\\cdot T(t)+{T}^{'}(t)\\cdot T(t) & =0 \\\\ 2{T}^{'}(t)\\cdot T(t) & =0 \\\\ {T}^{'}(t)\\cdot T(t) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, provided that $\\mathbf T'(t)\\neq \\mathbf 0,$ then it must be the case that $\\mathbf T'(t)$ is perpendicular to $\\mathbf T(t).$\n\nThe vector $\\mathbf T'(t)$ measures the rate of change of $\\mathbf T(t).$ However, since $\\mathbf T(t)$ is a unit vector for all $t,$ it changes only in its direction. The vector $\\mathbf T'(t)$ measures this rate of change in direction. \n\nTo form the principal normal $\\mathbf N(t),$ we normalize $\\mathbf T'(t),$ which leads to the definition\n\n$$\n\\mathbf N(t) = \\dfrac{\\mathbf T'(t)}{||\\mathbf T'(t)||}.\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "bd9190b9-f59c-5c06-987a-c6269ec9b3a7" :tutorial/math-academy-id 9377 :tutorial/title "Deriving the Formula for the Principal Normal"} :step/id #uuid "ee849a7a-e449-5317-872e-183c46fabb72" :step/math-academy-id 164586 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120645}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120645 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119787 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119534 :question/id #uuid "f5e6498b-73f9-5760-81bb-14e9acc495f8" :question/math-academy-id "e-14998"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "01fe665f-05e8-5183-9c98-cb760af0ae02" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120520} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120521} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262795} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262799} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186345083} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186345091} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186398312} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186398319} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186398326}] :knowledge-point/title "Calculating a Principal Normal Given Some Information About a Curve"} :step/id #uuid "8e583ccb-952c-52c6-b8a9-e4b716f0d3c4" :step/math-academy-id 164587 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120646}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120646 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186121150 :tutorial/content "Consider the straight line given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+t\\,[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ 0 \\\\ \\sqrt{2}\\end{aligned}],\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nIf we attempt to calculate its principal normal vector, we will get an interesting result. First, we calculate $\\mathbf r'(t)$ and $\\|\\mathbf r'(t)\\|\n\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{'}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[\\begin{aligned}-2+5t \\\\ 1 \\\\ \\sqrt{2}t\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ 0 \\\\ \\sqrt{2}\\end{aligned}] \\\\ ‖{r}^{'}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{{5}^{2}+(\\sqrt{2})^{2}}=\\sqrt{27}=3\\sqrt{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the unit tangent vector is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T(t) & =\\frac{{r}^{'}(t)}{‖{r}^{'}(t)‖}=\\frac{1}{3\\sqrt{3}}\\,[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ 0 \\\\ \\sqrt{2}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that in the case of a straight line, $\\mathbf T(t)$ is simply the (normalized) direction vector, as we would expect. \n\nNext, we calculate $\\mathbf T'(t)\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{T}^{'}(t) & =\\frac{1}{3\\sqrt{3}}\\,[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(5) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(0) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\sqrt{2})\\end{aligned}]=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $\\mathbf{T}'(t)$ is the zero vector, we can't normalize it as usual. If we try to apply the usual formula we get an undefined result due to division by zero:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}N(t) & =\\frac{{T}^{'}(t)}{‖{T}^{'}(t)‖}=\\frac{0}{‖0‖}=\\frac{0}{0}=\\text{undefined}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, a straight line does not have a principal normal.\n\nThe intuition behind this result is that the principal normal points in the direction in which a curve is bending, but a straight line does not bend at all." :tutorial/id #uuid "5c27df4b-0d17-5407-997b-c240d492eeca" :tutorial/math-academy-id 9378 :tutorial/title "Lines Do Not Have Principal Normal Vectors"} :step/id #uuid "1aa2d247-5dd4-5fdf-a44a-9cf0a8cd1e0d" :step/math-academy-id 164590}] :activity/title "Principal Normal Vectors" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186120013}]}
